---
// Selling food: markets, events and food trucks (#642, part of #277).
// Answers findability q11. Health department applications by type, the fee
// schedule, and the City of Morgantown rule for vending trucks and carts.
// Only the temporary event fee is stated: the rest of the FY 2026-2027 fee
// schedule is a scanned form that is hard to read. Waits for David (fees).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { fixFormUrl } from '../lib/group-requests.mjs';
import ResourceCard from '../components/ResourceCard.astro';
import SourceLine from '../components/SourceLine.astro';
import { resource, telHref } from '../lib/content.mjs';
export const page = { door: "family", topic: "work", label: "Selling food at markets, events and food trucks", summary: "Which permit fits a fair, a farmers market, a bake sale, food made in your home kitchen (cottage food) or a food truck, and the city rule for trucks and carts" };
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const eh = [{ url: 'https://www.monchd.org/departments-programs/environmental-health', checked: '2026-10-30' }];
const temp = [{ url: 'https://www.monchd.org/services/temporary-food-worker-cards', checked: '2026-10-30' }];
const cottage = [{ url: 'https://agriculture.wv.gov/divisions/regulatory-and-environmental-affairs/potentially-hazardous-cottage-foods/', checked: '2026-11-01' }, { url: 'https://code.wvlegislature.gov/19-40-1/', checked: '2026-11-01' }, { url: 'https://code.wvlegislature.gov/19-35-6/', checked: '2026-11-01' }, { url: 'https://agriculture.wv.gov/wp-content/uploads/Filed-Cottage-Food-Rule-Emergency-Rule-2026.pdf', checked: '2026-11-01' }, { url: 'https://agriculture.wv.gov/wp-content/uploads/West-Virginia-Department-of-Agriculture-Guide-for-Homemade-Food-Products.pdf', checked: '2026-11-01' }, { url: 'https://www.wvlegislature.gov/Bill_Status/Bills_history.cfm?input=44&year=2026&sessiontype=RS&btype=bill', checked: '2026-11-01' }];
const vending = [{ url: 'https://www.morgantownwv.gov/256/Vending-Downtown', checked: '2026-10-30' }];
const r = (slug) => resource(slug);
---
<Base title="Selling food in Morgantown and Mon County: markets, events, bake sales and food trucks" description="Which Monongalia County Health Department permit fits selling food at a fair or festival, a farmers market, a bake sale, from your home kitchen (cottage food), or from a food truck or cart, the fee schedule, and the City of Morgantown business license rule for vending trucks and carts.">
  <h1>Selling food at markets, events and food trucks</h1>
  <p class="lede">The Monongalia County Health Department handles food permits in Mon County. Which permit you need depends on where and how you sell. If you sell from a truck or cart in Morgantown, the city also needs you to have a business license. Read each office's rules, and call them if you are not sure. We link the rules and do not decide what applies to you.</p>

  <h2>A fair, festival or one-time event</h2>
  {r('monchd-temporary-food-permit') && <ResourceCard r={r('monchd-temporary-food-permit')} showCost />}

  <h2>A farmers market</h2>
  {r('monchd-farmers-market-vendor-permit') && <ResourceCard r={r('monchd-farmers-market-vendor-permit')} />}
  <p>Selling produce, canned foods or plants you grew? See <a href={`${base}/sell-what-you-grow/`}>Sell what you grow</a>.</p>

  <h2>A bake sale, or a nonprofit selling food</h2>
  <p>The health department's Environmental Health page posts a Bake Sale Application and an Exemption Application. Its event page says to call with questions about bake sales or nonprofit status: <a href={telHref('304-598-5131')}>304-598-5131</a>.</p>
  <p>The bake sale rules, as a short list: <a href={`${base}/bake-sale-rules/`}>Holding a bake sale</a>.</p>
  <p><a class="btn" href="https://www.monchd.org/departments-programs/environmental-health"><span>Health department applications</span></a></p>
  <SourceLine sources={[...eh, ...temp]} org="monongalia-county-health-department" about="Bake sale and nonprofit food applications" />

  <h2 id="home-kitchen">Food you make in your home kitchen (cottage food)</h2>
  <p>Baking cookies or cakes at home to sell, or making jam, salsa or other food? West Virginia law calls food made at the maker's home, and packed there if it is packed, and sold straight to the buyer "cottage food." It does not include meat, poultry, seafood or Grade A dairy products.</p>
  <p>The West Virginia Department of Agriculture (WVDA) says foods that are not "potentially hazardous" can be sold as cottage foods with no cottage food permit. Potentially hazardous foods need a WVDA permit first, and WVDA inspects your kitchen before it gives one. Its list of foods that need a permit includes salsas, sauces, pickled and fermented foods, canned acidified foods and cut produce; for some foods it says "Contact WVDA." Permits run January 1 through December 31. Cottage foods may be sold only inside West Virginia.</p>
  <p>The permit for potentially hazardous foods comes from Senate Bill 44, which the Governor signed on March 27, 2026. It took effect June 12, 2026.</p>
  <p><strong>Labels.</strong> State law says food that is not potentially hazardous must be labeled to WVDA's standards. For potentially hazardous food, WVDA's 2026 rule says the label must show the food's common name; the name of the maker, packer or seller; the city, state and ZIP; the ingredients, starting with "Ingredients:" and listed from most to least by weight; the amount in the package; any major allergens, right under the ingredients, starting with "Contains"; handling steps such as "keep refrigerated" when needed; and any date or lot code the rules require. It must also say: "This product was made in a non-commercial or home kitchen that may not be subject to inspection and may contain cross-contact allergens not included in the allergen statement." The rule says to send your labels to WVDA for review. WVDA's guide says label review is free.</p>
  <p><strong>Selling online.</strong> State law says food that is not potentially hazardous can be sold to the buyer in person or remotely, and delivered by you, someone working for you, a store that sells it for you, or a shipping company. We have not found a page that says whether potentially hazardous cottage food can be sold online.</p>
  <p><strong>Local rules.</strong> State law says counties and cities may not ban or regulate making and selling food that is not potentially hazardous. It also says that limit does not cover renting space at places the government owns or runs, events the government runs or approves, or events that last 14 days or less. WVDA's guide says state or local business licenses still apply.</p>
  <p>Not sure which kind your food is? Ask WVDA at <a href={telHref('304-558-2227')}>304-558-2227</a> or <a href="mailto:cottagefood@wvda.us">cottagefood@wvda.us</a>. We have not found a Mon County Health Department or City of Morgantown page about selling food made at home yet.</p>
  <p>The state guide's full lists of which foods need a permit, the label rules and the steps, as a short list: <a href={`${base}/cottage-food/`}>selling food made at home</a>.</p>
  <p><a class="btn" href="https://agriculture.wv.gov/divisions/regulatory-and-environmental-affairs/potentially-hazardous-cottage-foods/"><span>WVDA cottage food rules</span></a></p>
  <SourceLine sources={cottage} org="wv-department-of-agriculture" about="Cottage food rules" />

  <h2>A food truck, cart, restaurant or shop</h2>
  {r('monchd-food-business-plan-review') && <ResourceCard r={r('monchd-food-business-plan-review')} />}

  <h2>Selling from a truck or cart in Morgantown</h2>
  <p>The City of Morgantown says vending trucks and vending carts both need a City of Morgantown Business License, even if the business is based outside city limits. The city says a permit is not needed if you sell on private property with the landowner's permission. The city's page has maps of the downtown spaces used for vending permits. Questions about applying, or about selling from a truck or cart between 11 p.m. and 6 a.m.: Finance Department, <a href={telHref('304-284-7423')}>304-284-7423</a>.</p>
  <p><a class="btn" href="https://www.morgantownwv.gov/256/Vending-Downtown"><span>City vending rules</span></a></p>
  <SourceLine sources={vending} about="Morgantown vending trucks and carts" />

  <h2>Fees</h2>
  <p>The health department posts its fee list, "Permit Fees and Fees for Service, Fiscal Year 2026-2027." It lists $72 for a temporary food establishment. For other food permits, read the list or call Environmental Health before you pay. You can pay the health department online.</p>
  <p><a class="btn" href="https://cdn.sanity.io/files/95aan9ec/production/9af55503d7848dd4ca2ca580602065966a1d1bcf.pdf"><span>Fee list (PDF)</span></a></p>
  <SourceLine sources={[{ url: 'https://cdn.sanity.io/files/95aan9ec/production/9af55503d7848dd4ca2ca580602065966a1d1bcf.pdf', checked: '2026-10-30' }, ...eh]} org="monongalia-county-health-department" about="Health department fees" />

  <p>Starting a food business? See <a href={`${base}/business/`}>Start or grow a business</a> for free coaching and classes.</p>

  <p class="meta">Sources checked October 30, 2026. Rules and fees can change, so check the office's page before you sell or pay. Something out of date? <a href={fixFormUrl({ page: Astro.url.pathname })}>Tell us</a>.</p>
</Base>
